THE ANSWER IS "IPC IS COMPARABLE — BETTER, ACTUALLY — AND IT REALLY IS
INSTRUCTION COUNT". The port's IPC is ABOVE C's on every cell measured
(3.51 vs 3.01 at p2, 3.44 vs 2.83 at p6, 3.58 vs 2.70 on the screenshot),
and per instruction the port misses the data cache HALF as often, reaches
DRAM no more often, and mispredicts FEWER branches than C. There is no
stall excess to find: the port's 875 M extra instructions at photo_cid p6
cost 212 M extra cycles, which is LESS than those instructions would cost at
the port's own average IPC. The Ir-based ranking is therefore the honest
picture of the residual, and three lines of investigation close at once —
cache locality, branch misprediction and `#[cold]` are all measured NOT to
be the gap. What DOES change is the ORDERING: ranked by cycles instead of
Ir, the allocator/zero-fill traffic moves to #1, and `cost_coeffs_txb` —
which the Ir ranking listed as a 1.18x port LOSS — is the port's single
biggest WIN, 0.71x C's cycles.

