  * callgrind cells: `ident.tsv` records ident=Y plus the instrumented run's
    OWN .obu cmp'd against the pre-pass's (port and C) on all three.
  * The `target-dbg` (line-tables) binary's output was cmp'd against the
    plain release binary's at photo_cid p6: identical.

Independent cross-check against the existing record: this run's callgrind Ir
totals reproduce `compute_stats_x86_recheck_2026-09-05` to the unit —
photo_cid p6 port 1,619,432,103 (that record: 1,619,433,293, a 1,190-Ir /
0.00007 % difference from the cache-sim instrumentation), C 705,587,387 vs
705,588,241; screen_terminal p6 port 920,898,277 vs 920,899,041, C
355,383,974 vs 355,384,782. The p2 Ir ratio, 46,002,891,962 / 27,248,546,356
= 1.688, is the 1.688x this chunk's brief quoted.

=================== 1. THE HEADLINE: perf stat, PORT vs C ===================

Method: `perf stat` over the whole process at TWO warmup levels and the
PAIRED DELTA taken per repetition, so process setup, .yuv read and .obu
write cancel EXACTLY and what remains is N encodes' worth of counters
(p2: warmup 0 and 2, delta = 2 encodes; p6: warmup 1 and 9, delta = 8).
Median of 5 repetitions per (cell, side, event group); three event groups
because Zen 4 exposes six general PMCs. Both harnesses take `warmup` with
the same meaning (N untimed full cycles, then one more).

  cell                instructions/encode        cycles/encode         IPC
                       port          C     x     port        C     x   port   C    x
  photo_cid p2    45,756,333,894 26,106,293,236 1.753 13,052,055,418 8,666,419,352 1.506  3.506 3.012 1.164
  photo_cid p6     1,542,313,111    667,616,040 2.310    447,690,666   235,758,658 1.899  3.445 2.832 1.217
  screen_term p6     855,501,246    334,779,042 2.555    238,676,684   123,797,100 1.928  3.584 2.704 1.325

READ THE LAST TWO COLUMN GROUPS TOGETHER. The port executes 1.75-2.56x C's
instructions but spends only 1.51-1.93x C's cycles, because it retires
16-33 % more instructions per cycle. Cycles on a fixed core are the
wall-clock quantity; **the port's real-time gap is 14 % (p2), 18 % (p6) and
25 % (screenshot) SMALLER than its instruction gap.** The uninstrumented
harness clocks agree: a single p6 run reads ENCODE_NS 92,774,387 (port) vs
48,679,611 (C) = 1.906x against the 1.899x cycle ratio, and p2 reads
2,419,359,687 vs 1,561,195,919 = 1.550x against 1.506x.

The arithmetic that closes the stall hypothesis, photo_cid p6: the port
spends 211,932,008 more cycles than C for 874,697,071 more instructions.
At the port's OWN average IPC those instructions would cost 253,900,000
cycles. The excess is CHEAPER than the port's average work, not dearer.
There is no stall excess to attribute.

=================== 2. QUESTION 1 — CACHE LOCALITY: NO ===================

Hardware, per 1000 instructions (PSTAT_DERIVED rows):

  metric                     photo_cid p2      photo_cid p6    screen_term p6
                             port     C        port     C       port     C
  L1D load-miss MPKI         2.159  5.533     4.559  10.117    5.856  14.492
  L1D load-miss rate (%)     0.745  1.543     1.583   2.909    2.159   4.236
  L1I miss MPKI              0.895  0.910     0.511   0.752    0.527   0.870
  op-cache miss MPKI        31.202 30.891    18.467  30.414   21.072  38.318
  DRAM/IO fills PKI         0.0003 0.0028   (noise) 0.0054  (noise)  0.246

The port is BETTER on every cache axis per instruction, and on the two p6
cells its `ls_any_fills_from_sys.all_dram_io` delta is NEGATIVE — i.e. the
per-encode DRAM traffic is below the measurement noise floor on the port
side while C's is a small positive number. Nothing on either side is
DRAM-bound: at photo_cid p6 the deterministic simulation counts 128,472
last-level misses for the port against 132,781 for C, on frames of 1.62 G
and 0.71 G instructions.

Deterministic confirmation (valgrind cache sim, per 1000 Ir):

  cell               D1 miss (rd+wr) PKI     LL miss (rd+wr) absolute
                      port      C              port       C
  photo_cid p2        1.607    3.545         138,988    196,005
  photo_cid p6        3.542    6.919         128,472    132,781
  screen_term p6      4.356   10.116         128,596    242,544

Two caveats stated rather than hidden. (a) In ABSOLUTE terms the port takes
FEWER L1D load-misses than C at p2 (98,789,820 vs 144,447,284) and about the
SAME at p6 (7,030,730 vs 6,754,460) and on the screenshot (5,009,394 vs
4,851,608) — so the per-instruction advantage is not an artefact of the
port executing more instructions, but the per-instruction framing is still
the honest one to compare with. (b)
callgrind's I1 model has no op-cache and reads the port's instruction-fetch
misses as WORSE (7.74 vs 12.72 PKI at p6 favours the port, but 15.62 vs
13.54 at p2 does not); the hardware, which has the op-cache, reads L1I
misses as equal at p2 (0.895 vs 0.910 MPKI) and better for the port at p6.
Instruction misses that reach the last level are negligible on both sides
(port 9,938 / C 11,593 at p6). Trust the hardware.

TLBs are not a cost anywhere: the largest absolute figure is the port's
227,013 dTLB load-misses per p2 encode (0.005 per 1000 instructions) against
C's 33,572 — a 6.76x ratio on a number too small to matter, and at p6 the
port is BELOW C on both dTLB (5,675 vs 10,468) and iTLB (652 vs 5,116).

=================== 3. QUESTION 2 — BRANCH MISPREDICTION: NO, AND NO `#[cold]` ===================

Hardware, per encode:

  cell             branches            branch-misses        miss rate      MPKI
                   port        C     x   port      C      x  port   C     port   C
  photo_cid p2  7,526,300,395 2,470,438,082 3.047 91,029,444 84,616,190 1.076 1.209% 3.425% 1.989 3.241
  photo_cid p6    249,836,504    61,261,915 4.078  3,825,197  2,271,030 1.684 1.531% 3.707% 2.480 3.402
  screen_term p6  138,947,816    36,751,241 3.781  1,276,958    828,219 1.542 0.919% 2.254% 1.493 2.474

The port takes 1.08-1.68x C's absolute mispredicts while executing 1.75-2.56x
C's instructions, so per instruction it mispredicts LESS than C on every
cell — and by rate, 2-3x less. Mispredicts are not where the port loses.

What the branch counters DO show is a structural signature: the port executes
3.0-4.1x C's BRANCH INSTRUCTIONS. Branch density is 16.45 % / 16.20 % /
16.24 % of the port's instructions against C's 9.46 % / 9.18 % / 10.98 % —
one branch every 6.1 port instructions against one every 9.1-10.9 C
instructions. That is the shape of shorter basic blocks: retained bounds
checks, `Option` tests, per-element loop control where C runs straight-line
SIMD. It costs INSTRUCTIONS (see §5), and the predictor absorbs it.

**NO `#[cold]` CANDIDATE IS WARRANTED, and the evidence is specific.** The
largest single mispredicting site in the port is `pipeline.rs:9333` —
316,928 simulated mispredicts on 1,846,784 conditional branches, a 17.16 %
rate, which is 82.4 % of `encode_block_syntax`'s 384,736 and 3.4 % of the
port's whole frame. It is:

    9331    let mut eob = 0i32;
    9332    for (i, &pos) in scan.iter().enumerate() {
    9333        if coeffs[pos as usize] != 0 {
    9334            eob = i as i32 + 1;
    9335        }
    9336    }

a roughly even data-dependent test inside a FORWARD scan of the whole
transform block with no early exit. `#[cold]` marks a RARE path; this branch
is not rare, so the attribute cannot help it. The fix is the one already in
this repo: `quant.rs:318-324`'s `eob_from_qcoeff` walks the scan in REVERSE
and returns on the first non-zero — its branch at `quant.rs:320` mispredicts
at 3.10 % against this one's 17.16 %, and it executes a fraction of the
iterations. The identical forward loop is duplicated at
`pipeline.rs:9410-9415` for the tx_depth>0 path.

The other high-rate sites are all RD decisions inside RDOQ — `quant.rs:1127`
(45.15 % on 1,210,757 branches), `:1143` (34.52 %), `:1195` (31.50 %),
`:1156` (28.40 %) — and C's `svt_av1_optimize_b` has exactly the same shape
(10.42 % over 12,854,248 branches). On hardware the two sides' per-encode
mispredict counts in this function are within 5 % of each other (port
1,033,131 vs C 988,622 at photo_cid p6). Nothing to mark cold; the branches
are genuinely unpredictable on both sides.

CAVEAT ON THE SIMULATED NUMBERS, stated because they disagree with hardware
in SIGN: callgrind models a small two-level predictor, not Zen 4's. Its
frame totals read the port as mispredicting MORE per instruction than C
(5.70 vs 4.78 per 1000 Ir at photo_cid p6) while the hardware reads the
opposite (2.48 vs 3.40 MPKI). Use the simulation only to LOCALISE a
mispredict inside the port, which it does well — its
`encode_block_syntax` total (411,179 including indirect) lands within 33 % of
the hardware's 308,170 for the same function — and use the hardware for any
port-vs-C claim.

